well the strip continues to evolve mgm  resorts announcing it's selling the  mirage to hard rock international for  one billion dollars kate houston spoke  to a long time mirage employee about how  this purchase could possibly impact jobs  i change sometimes is a good thing so  you know we welcome that change aaron  najera has worked at the mirage for 30  years and has gone through ownership  changes including when mgm resorts  acquired the property in 2000. right now  nobody's really worried because again  we've been through this and then you  know the first couple times we were  nervous  but not not anymore nahra says  historically these changes have not  impacted jobs nor benefits for those our  union we are you know at ease for now  you know we do  think about our brothers and sisters  that are not union  that hopefully there is no changes in  their employment in a letter to  employees mirage president nick  ritterstrom said there would not be  changes to jobs or daily operations from  now until the sale closes in mid-2022  mgm resorts will keep the mirage name  and brand licensing it to hard rock for  a few years as they finalize plans to  rebrand the property this rendering from  hard rock shows its plan to build a  guitar-shaped hotel next to the main  property it's unclear if this will take  over any current attractions including  the famous volcano nahara says they're  waiting to see what happens changes are  always good and so far the mirage has  always made good moves kate houston live  local now  as a part of the agreement the mirage  executive team is expected to transition  to hard rock they will hold a meeting  with hard rock reps to share more and  more information with employees and  answer any questions that might come up  [Music]
 

